course: dictionary
lesson: 10776
title: What is a vaccine?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A vaccine is a medicine that teaches your body to fight a particular disease. It is usually given as an injection, which people call a jab or a shot. «At the yearly check-up a dog gets an injection, a vaccine that teaches its body to fight dangerous illnesses before it ever meets them.» The vaccine prepares the body in advance.
explain: Use the context to work out what a word means. The sentence says the vaccine protects you from serious illness, so it helps you stay well. Ask yourself what word would fit. Try «medicine» in place of «vaccine»: «The medicine protects you from serious illness» still makes sense, so the meaning is right.
explain: Some words mean the same thing in different places. In Britain a vaccination is often called a «jab», and in the USA people say «get a shot». «A sore arm, a slight fever or tiredness for a day or two after a jab is a normal sign the immune system is responding.» A sore arm after a jab is normal and does not mean you have the disease.
fact: A vaccine usually contains a harmless, weak or dead form of a germ, or instructions to copy part of it.
fact: Your immune system learns to recognise the germ and makes antibodies, so your body is ready if the real germ comes.
fact: «In 1796 Edward Jenner made the first vaccine from cowpox.» It was used against smallpox.
fact: Smallpox was wiped off the whole Earth, and this was declared done in 1980.
fact: Children get vaccines against measles, mumps and whooping cough, and many people get a flu vaccine each year.
exercise: «At the yearly check-up a dog gets an injection, a vaccine that teaches its body to fight dangerous illnesses.» What is a vaccine? | A type of cold weather | A bandage for a cut | *A medicine that teaches your body to fight a disease | A sweet you eat when you feel sad
exercise: Before a jab, «you can tell the nurse you are scared, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand». What does jab mean? | A long walk to school | A cold drink | *An injection, often a vaccination
exercise: «A sore arm after a jab is a normal sign the immune system is responding.» What does a sore arm after a jab show? | You must stay in bed for a month | *Your body is responding and learning | You have caught the disease | The vaccine has failed
test: Why did governments ask people to wear masks and stay home in 2020 and 2021? | *To slow the spread before there were vaccines | Because masks are a type of medicine | To stop people from going to school forever | To make the vaccines work faster
test: Who made the first vaccine, from cowpox? | Isaac Newton | *Edward Jenner | Louis Armstrong | Marie Curie
exam: What does a vaccine teach your body to do? | *Recognise a germ and be ready to fight it | Sleep for a whole day | Grow taller quickly | Lose all of its germs
